        "math_academy_id": "q-320731",
        "problem": "Write $49{r}^{2}+9$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$49{r}^{2}+9=$\n{{field-1}}",
        "worked_solution": "To factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49{r}^{2}+9 & =49{r}^{2}-(-9) \\\\  & =49{r}^{2}-(9{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(7r)^{2}-(3i)^{2} \\\\  & =(7r+3i)(7r-3i)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
        "deliberately_incorrect_submission": false,
